# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, and it is not about caching.
#
# `wrangler.jsonc` sets `run_worker_first: ["/api/*", "/auth/*"]`, so /assets/*
# is served by Cloudflare's static-asset path and NEVER reaches worker/index.ts.
# `withSecurityHeaders` — which sets COOP/COEP — therefore never runs on an
# asset. The comment beside that config reasoned about the DOCUMENT ("HTML is
# always rendered by the worker, so every document response carries the
# COOP/COEP headers") and it is correct about documents. It missed the WORKER
# SCRIPT.
#
# A dedicated worker started from a cross-origin-isolated document must itself
# be delivered with `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp`, or it fails
# to start. The slicer kernel is loaded exactly that way, from /assets/. So the
# document was isolated, the kernel chunk was not, and the worker never booted.
#
# The browser reports that failure as a bare `Event` with NO message, and
# three-slicer's own handler falls back to `ne.message || "worker error"` and
# prints «Worker terminated (likely out of memory): worker error». It is not a
# memory problem and never was — measured: 3 ms to the banner with nothing
# loaded, versus a 343 ms kernel boot once this header is present.
#
# It is inert where it is not needed. COEP on a script, stylesheet or wasm
# subresource is only consulted when the response creates a new environment;
# for everything else the browser ignores it. And when the owning document is
# NOT isolated — which is the case on the iPad, where worker/index.ts
# deliberately withholds isolation so the engine takes its single-threaded
# core — a worker script that asserts require-corp is still allowed. The check
# only fails in the other direction. So this file does not disturb that
# mitigation.
#
# CORP is `same-site` rather than `same-origin` on purpose: it satisfies COEP
# just as well, and it leaves levonis-iq.com able to load a studio asset if it
# ever needs to. `same-origin` would have broken that silently.
#
# vinext writes this file itself ONLY when it does not already exist
# (node_modules/vinext/dist/index.js, the `_headers` block), so committing it
# here replaces the generated one — which means the Cache-Control line below
# must stay. Dropping it would un-cache every hashed asset.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-site
